.TH TIMBERVIEW 1 "2026-08-29" "timberfs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timberview \- read a timberfs store as a tape
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timberview
.RI [ --resolver " CMD" ]
.RI [ --targets " FILE" ]
.RI [ --cmd " ARGV" ]
.RI [ --hosts " H,H" ]
.RI [ --at " TIME" ]
.RI [ --chunk " N" ]
.RI [ TARGET ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A pager over a store. It opens the last chunk and scrolls back from
there, with no predicate and no result set: where
.BR timbersh (1)
walks an ANSWER, this walks the LOG.
.PP
It does no entry parsing \(em no timestamps, no window verification, no
token index. Chunks, decompressed, shown as lines. That is the feature:
the two stores a query serves worst, one whose lines cannot be parsed
and one with no index, are exactly the ones you most want to look at,
and this asks nothing of the content.
.PP
.B TARGET
is a store name, a path, a short id, or a
.B timber:
address. With none, the first store is opened and
.B S
switches between the rest.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-resolver " CMD"
A command that prints the fleet as a target document \(em a name and the
argv that reaches it, per target. See
.BR timbersh (1),
WHERE THE FLEET IS, for the document and the order the sources are tried
in.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_RESOLVER )
.TP
.BI \-\-targets " FILE"
The same document, from a file.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_TARGETS )
.TP
.BI \-\-cmd " ARGV"
One command reaching every host, with
.B _TIMBERHOST_
substituted per
.BR \-\-hosts .
It is handed a
.BR timberfs\-query\-document (5)
on stdin. Default
.BR "timberfs query --query -" .
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_CMD )
.TP
.BI \-\-hosts " H,H"
The hosts that command reaches. A search covers all of them.
.RB ( $TIMBERFS_HOSTS )
.TP
.BI \-\-at " TIME"
Open at the chunk covering this instant. RFC3339,
.BR "YYYY-MM-DD [HH:MM[:SS]]" ,
.B HH:MM[:SS]
for today, or unix seconds \(em resolved HERE, in the reader's timezone.
.TP
.BI \-\-chunk " N"
Open at this chunk number.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BR j " " k " " \(ua " " \(da
A line.
.BR g "/" G
go to the log's top and end \(em a seek, not a walk back.
.TP
.BR space " " b
A page.
.BR h " " l " " \(<- " " \(->
scroll sideways when lines are not wrapped;
.B w
toggles wrapping. Neither is a default that can be argued into being
correct, so it changes at runtime.
.TP
.BR Tab " " Shift\-Tab
Move between the searchable TERMS on the line \(em the widest identifier
at each position, separators and all.
.PP
.RS
A term is not an index token. The index holds alphanumeric runs of 3 to
64 characters, so a UUID is five of them and none of them is the id; a
.B has
term may carry the separators an identifier is written with, and
timberfs ANDs the runs inside it before matching the whole thing
word-anchored. So the UUID is ONE term, and the one worth offering: its
pieces are far less selective. Runs are joined on
.BR - " " . " " _ " " :
but never across
.B =
or
.BR / ,
which separate fields rather than sit inside a name.
.PP
A term the index cannot hold at all \(em
.BR 26.1.18 ,
three runs of under three characters \(em is refused where you point at
it rather than discovered later as an empty answer.
.RE
.TP
.BR Enter " " *
Search the picked token everywhere: every store, on every host.
.B /
searches one you type, and
.BR n "/" N
cycle the hits. The hit list itself takes the same keys:
.B Tab
walks the terms of the highlighted hit and
.B *
searches that one, so an identifier can be followed without opening
anything first.
.B Enter
there still goes to the hit.
.TP
.B y
Show this line's address.
.B S
opens another store.
.B ?
lists the keys.
.B q
quits, printing the address you stopped at.
.SH AN ANSWER INSTEAD OF A STORE
A
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
stream on standard input is paged as an ANSWER rather than a store:
.PP
.RS
.EX
timberfs query --records --from 13:00 --has ERROR app.log | timberview
.EE
.RE
.PP
timbersh reaches the same screen with
.BR "select ... into view" .
An entry record carries the chunk and offset it came from, so every line
in an answer knows its place:
.B Tab
and the search work as they do anywhere \(em
.B Enter
searches the picked term on an answer exactly as it does on the tape \(em
and
.B o
leaves the answer for the LOG around that entry, which is what you
usually want next.
.PP
A multi-line entry is ONE entry: the lines of a stack trace belong to
the entry that raised it. An answer is a closed set, so both ends are
ends and the boundary says so rather than naming a chunk. An entry still
at the live edge is shown and cannot be opened, because it is in no
chunk yet \(em counted and said, never dropped. On a store being written
that is ordinary: the newest matches are still in the write-ahead
sidecar.
.PP
⚠ A target too old to place an entry is a DIFFERENT fact.
.B offset
on an entry record landed in 0.26.0, so a target still on 0.25.0 answers
with entries that have no place at all. Calling that a live edge would
be a claim about the data where the truth is about the server, so the
stream's own
.B server_version
decides, and the note names the target and what to upgrade past.
.PP
⚠ Reading from a pipe needs a keyboard from somewhere other than stdin,
which is the answer:
.I /dev/tty
is reopened for keys, as every pager does.
.SH THE ADDRESS
A coordinate is passed from a search to a viewer and back to a shell, so
it has a written form:
.PP
.RS
.EX
timber://mail01/79d7f23a-b044-4a72-8be3-d26e0481d202#offset=33724753900
timber://mail01/79d7f23a-\&...#chunk=498248
timber:79d7f23a-\&...
.EE
.RE
.PP
The store id is the NAME and the host is a hint: bake the host in and a
pasted link breaks the day a store moves. A position says which
coordinate it is, because
.B #offset=
and
.B #chunk=
are different numbers and one that resolved to the wrong place silently
would be worse than one that will not parse. Prefer an offset: it is
absolute on the tape, so it stays valid until retention drops the data
and then the store's own
.B first_seq
says it was dropped rather than "not found".
.SH WAITING
A pager over a fleet spends real time on round trips. Every target is
asked at once rather than in turn, the chunks either side of where you
landed are read ahead while you read the one you are on, and a chunk
once fetched is cached \(em safe because a chunk's bytes never change
after it is written.
.PP
What is left says so: a read that takes longer than a moment paints what
it is waiting for, on which host, and for how long.
.B ^C
gives up on that answer and returns you to the screen, rather than
ending the session \(em so a host that has stopped responding cannot
strand you.
.SH WHEN SOMETHING IS DOWN
A target that does not answer is NAMED and the rest are still asked \(em
one machine being down must not stop you reading the logs that are
available. It is said in each of the three places the failure would
otherwise be invisible: an unreachable fleet reports that nothing was
LISTED rather than that there is no store, a store that was not found
names who was not asked (it may be on exactly that host), and a chunk
that could not be read says so where the boundary marker goes, so a
scroll that stopped is not the same screen as the end of the log.
.SH BOUNDARIES
Retention moves the floor while you read, and the newest lines are in no
chunk yet. Both are stated: the top says whether it is the top of the
LOG or only of what is loaded, and how much fell off; the bottom names
the writer, so the end of the tape is not mistaken for the end of the
log.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on a normal exit;
.B 1
where no store could be opened or no chunk found there.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timbersh (1),
.BR timberfs (1),
.BR timberfs\-query\-document (5),
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
